# Development server on Kubernetes

This journey runs one `all` replica: one pod that serves HTTP, the UI, and
gRPC and runs every Bifrost role (Scribe, Oracle, Forge) in-process. It is the
shape to use for a shared development or staging cluster. For several
replicas, see [Production server on Kubernetes](/self-hosting/kubernetes-production/).
To run it on your laptop instead, start with [Local development](/self-hosting/local-development/).

## Prerequisites

- A Kubernetes cluster, `kubectl`, and a namespace (this page uses `wyrd`).
- An external PostgreSQL 16 reachable from the cluster, and a login that owns
  the `wyrd` database.
- A default StorageClass for the pod's persistent volume.
- `openssl`, Docker, and a repository checkout with [`mise`](https://mise.jdx.dev/).
- A registry the cluster can pull from, or a local cluster (kind, minikube)
  you can load an image into.

## The image

No Wyrd image is published yet. Build the official image from the commit you
are deploying and record its immutable image ID:

```bash
mise run docker:build                  # release build of wyrd-server, tagged `wyrd`
docker image inspect -f '{{.Id}}' wyrd # sha256:… — record it with the commit
```

The manifests below take one of two image branches. Both workloads (the
migration Job and the StatefulSet) must use the same one:

| Cluster | `image:` | `imagePullPolicy:` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Local kind cluster, after `kind load docker-image wyrd --name <cluster>` | `wyrd:latest` | `Never` |
| A cluster that pulls from your registry, after `docker tag wyrd registry.example.com/wyrd && docker push registry.example.com/wyrd` | `registry.example.com/wyrd@sha256:…` (the pushed digest, not a tag) | omit it |

The manifests show the kind branch. For a registry, replace both lines. With
`Never`, a node without the loaded image reports `ErrImageNeverPull` instead
of fetching something else. Once a release is published, pin its published
digest instead of a local build.

The image listens on `8080` (nginx: API and UI) and `50051` (public gRPC), and
mounts its state volume at `/var/lib/wyrd`.

## Environment

| Variable | When | Value | Source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_DATABASE_URL` | Required | `postgresql://wyrd_tenant:…@<db-host>:5432/wyrd` | The tenant login you create. Secret `wyrd-db`. |
| `WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL` | Optional; this guide separates the logins | `postgresql://wyrd_platform:…@<db-host>:5432/wyrd` | The platform login you create; the migration Job connects as it. Secret `wyrd-db`. Unset, it defaults to `WYRD_DATABASE_URL` (see [Database](/self-hosting/database/#logins)). |
| `WYRD_STORAGE_URL` | Required | `file:///var/lib/wyrd/storage` | The pod's persistent volume. An object store (`s3://…`, `gs://…`, `az://…`) also works: see [Storage](/self-hosting/storage/). |
| `WYRD_BIFROST_DATA_DIR` | Set by the image | `/var/lib/wyrd/bifrost` | Scribe WAL, staging, and stable node identity. It lives on the same persistent volume. |
| `WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE` | Required for stable tokens | `/run/wyrd/signing.pem` | An Ed25519 key in Secret `wyrd-signing`. Without it a development pod mints an ephemeral key on every start. |
| `APP_ENV` | Optional | `development` (the default) | Use `staging` to exercise the hardened profile. |
| `WYRD_TARGET` | Optional | unset (`all`) | One process runs every role. |
| `WYRD_PEER_ADDRESS`, `WYRD_PEER_TLS_DIR` | Only for peer mode | unset | See [Adding replicas](#adding-replicas). |

## 1. Database logins and Secrets

Create the `wyrd_tenant` and `wyrd_platform` logins and their privileges once,
as described in [Database](/self-hosting/database/#logins). Passwords never go
on a command line, where other users of the machine and command auditing can
read them. Load both into shell variables from your password manager (for
example `read -rs TENANT_PW`), using URL-safe values such as
`openssl rand -hex 32`.

Generate the signing key and store every credential as a Secret. Each
`<(printf …)` hands `kubectl` a pipe to read, so the URL never appears in its
arguments or on disk. Keep a copy of each credential in your password manager;
the cluster is not the only record.

```bash
openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out signing.pem
kubectl create namespace wyrd
kubectl -n wyrd create secret generic wyrd-db \
  --from-file=database-url=<(printf %s "postgresql://wyrd_tenant:$TENANT_PW@<db-host>:5432/wyrd") \
  --from-file=platform-database-url=<(printf %s "postgresql://wyrd_platform:$PLATFORM_PW@<db-host>:5432/wyrd")
kubectl -n wyrd create secret generic wyrd-signing --from-file=signing.pem
rm signing.pem
```

## 2. Migrate with a one-off Job

The Job reads the same Secret the server does and connects as the platform login.

```yaml
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata: {name: wyrd-migrate, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  backoffLimit: 0
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
        - name: migrate
          image: wyrd:latest            # registry: registry.example.com/wyrd@sha256:…
          imagePullPolicy: Never        # registry: remove this line
          command: [wyrd-server, migrate]
          env:
            - name: WYRD_DATABASE_URL
              valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: wyrd-db, key: database-url}}
            - name: WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL
              valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: wyrd-db, key: platform-database-url}}
```

```bash
kubectl apply -f migrate.yaml
kubectl -n wyrd wait --for=condition=complete job/wyrd-migrate --timeout=300s
kubectl -n wyrd logs job/wyrd-migrate    # -> Wyrd and Vala schemas are migrated and valid.
kubectl -n wyrd delete job wyrd-migrate
```

Rerun the Job for each new image. `migrate` is idempotent.

## 3. Serve one `all` replica

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata: {name: wyrd, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  selector: {app: wyrd}
  ports:
    - {name: http, port: 8080}
    - {name: grpc, port: 50051}
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata: {name: wyrd, namespace: wyrd}
spec:
  serviceName: wyrd
  replicas: 1
  selector: {matchLabels: {app: wyrd}}
  template:
    metadata: {labels: {app: wyrd}}
    spec:
      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 45
      containers:
        - name: wyrd
          image: wyrd:latest            # registry: registry.example.com/wyrd@sha256:…
          imagePullPolicy: Never        # registry: remove this line
          env:
            - name: WYRD_DATABASE_URL
              valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: wyrd-db, key: database-url}}
            - name: WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL
              valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: wyrd-db, key: platform-database-url}}
            - {name: WYRD_STORAGE_URL, value: "file:///var/lib/wyrd/storage"}
            - {name: WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE, value: /run/wyrd/signing.pem}
          ports:
            - {name: http, containerPort: 8080}
            - {name: grpc, containerPort: 50051}
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet: {path: /readyz, port: http}
            periodSeconds: 2
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: http}
            periodSeconds: 10
          # The default `all` process needs at least 3.75 GiB.
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: 500m, memory: 1Gi}
            limits: {cpu: "2", memory: 4Gi}
          volumeMounts:
            - {name: data, mountPath: /var/lib/wyrd}
            - {name: signing, mountPath: /run/wyrd, readOnly: true}
      volumes:
        - name: signing
          secret: {secretName: wyrd-signing}
  volumeClaimTemplates:
    - metadata: {name: data}
      spec: {accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce], resources: {requests: {storage: 10Gi}}}
```

```bash
kubectl apply -f wyrd.yaml
kubectl -n wyrd rollout status statefulset/wyrd --timeout=300s
```

The pod is Ready once `/readyz` returns 200: the schema matches the binary,
storage answers, and the Bifrost roles are serving. `/healthz` only says the
process is up. If the volume mounts root-owned and the pod cannot write it,
set the pod's `securityContext.fsGroup` to the image's `wyrd` group
(`docker run --rm <image> id -g`).

### Routing

Clients reach gRPC at the same host as the HTTP URL, on port `50051`. Expose
both ports on one hostname: a LoadBalancer Service, or an ingress that
carries HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 on `8080` plus a TCP or gRPC route for `50051`.
For a quick check, forward both:

```bash
kubectl -n wyrd port-forward svc/wyrd 8080:8080 50051:50051
```

## 4. First-use setup

Run setup through `kubectl exec`, so the credentials print to your terminal
and never to the pod's log. Do not run it as a Job: a Job's output is its log.

```bash
kubectl -n wyrd exec wyrd-0 -- wyrd-server setup --tenant acme
```

It prints the platform root credential (`wyrd_global_…`) and the tenant's
`admin_credential: wyrd_sk_…`, each exactly once. Store both in your password
manager now. Rerunning setup for an existing tenant changes nothing and prints
no credential.

To create another tenant after the root exists, use the `wyrd` CLI on your
machine through the port-forward from step 3. Paste the stored root
credential at the silent prompt, so it stays in your shell and never reaches
a command line, the Kubernetes API, or a pod:

```bash
export WYRD_SERVER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080
read -rs WYRD_PLATFORM_CREDENTIAL && export WYRD_PLATFORM_CREDENTIAL
wyrd platform tenant create --slug beta --display-name "Beta"
```

It prints `beta`'s administrative credential once. Never pass a credential as
a `kubectl exec` argument: the API server can record it.

## 5. An authenticated client request

With the port-forward from step 3 running:

```bash
export WYRD_SERVER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080
export WYRD_API_KEY=wyrd_sk_…     # the admin_credential from setup
curl -s $WYRD_SERVER_URL/readyz
wyrd apply hello                  # the Card from Local development, step 5
wyrd list --kind Prompt
```

## Restart persistence

```bash
kubectl -n wyrd delete pod wyrd-0
kubectl -n wyrd rollout status statefulset/wyrd
wyrd list --kind Prompt           # the Card is still there; the API key still works
```

Postgres holds the registry, the volume claim `data-wyrd-0` holds artifacts and
Bifrost state, and the stable signing key keeps issued tokens valid. Deleting
the StatefulSet keeps the claim; deleting the claim loses local storage and
Bifrost data.

## Failure and recovery

| Symptom | Cause | Recovery |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The pod exits with `WYRD_SQL_503_SCHEMA_NOT_READY` | The schema is unmigrated, from another image, or its grants or policies drifted. | Run the migration Job for this image, then delete the pod. |
| The migration Job fails | A bad database URL, an unreachable database, or a failed migration. | Read `kubectl logs job/wyrd-migrate`, fix the cause, delete and reapply the Job. Serving pods keep refusing a mismatched schema rather than serving it. |
| The pod stays unready | A dependency is down. | `kubectl logs wyrd-0` names the failing check. |
| The pod is OOM-killed or refuses to boot on its memory budget | The limit is below the `all` floor. | Keep the limit at 3.75 GiB or more. |
| `setup` asks for `WYRD_PLATFORM_CREDENTIAL` | The platform root already exists. | Create the tenant with `wyrd platform tenant create`, as in step 4. If the root credential is lost, `kubectl exec wyrd-0 -- wyrd-server recover-root`. |

## Adding replicas

Scale out by adding `all` replicas first, and move to role-specific pods only
when one workload outgrows that shape. Every `all` replica serves ingest and
reads. Forge planning is a singleton held by a lease, so one replica plans while
the others stand by, ready, and take over if it stops; every replica still runs
compaction. More than one replica needs peer mode, which changes the storage and
networking requirements:

- `WYRD_PEER_ADDRESS` on every pod, from its own pod IP, and `WYRD_PEER_TLS_DIR`
  holding a dedicated CA and one shared `wyrd-peer` leaf.
- One **shared** object store. `file://` storage is process-local, so peers
  cannot share it.
- A persistent volume per Scribe replica for `WYRD_BIFROST_DATA_DIR`.
- A private peer port (`50052`) reachable only from other Wyrd pods.

[Production server on Kubernetes](/self-hosting/kubernetes-production/) walks
through that shape.

